<!--
prompt_v01.md.template — the initial prompt skeleton, six-section
XML structure per the prompt-architect sub-skill.

Produced by:  the designer agent during /spp-init, in collaboration
              with the user. The designer fills in Persona, Task,
              Rules (initial set, expected to grow), Output Format,
              and an Example Input / Example Output that demonstrate
              the format.
Consumed by:  /spp-loop iteration 1 reads this file as prompt_v01.md.
              Subsequent iterations write prompt_v02.md, prompt_v03.md,
              etc. into runs/<model>/run_NN/, evolving from this
              skeleton.
Mid-iteration evolution: each iteration's discrepancy analysis
                         proposes Rule edits. The auditor reviews the
                         diff (DESIGN.md §4.2) before it is committed
                         to the next iteration's prompt.

Six-section XML structure (Persona, Task, Rules, Output Format,
Example Input, Example Output) is the canonical prompt-architect
shape. Sections are XML tags so that the model has stable
structural anchors and so that later iterations' diffs are
section-scoped (e.g. "Rules grew by 2 entries; Persona unchanged").

Placeholder syntax: {{PLACEHOLDER_NAME}}, with inline HTML comments
describing what fills it. The Phase 4 template linter verifies all
placeholders are resolved before the file is treated as a final
prompt_v01.md.

DESIGN.md cross-references:
  §2.2  — model overfitting acceptance with documentation
  §4.2  — the auditor reviews Rule edits before iteration N+1 runs
  §10   — glossary (categorical vs row-specific rule edits)
-->

<!--
============================================================
MODEL-SPECIFIC DIRECTIVES (header)
============================================================
Optional. These are strings prepended (or appended, depending on
the model's conventions) outside the XML body. They are
**model-locked** — they only have meaning for one model family.

Examples:
  /no_think    — Qwen3 family directive that disables
                 chain-of-thought tokens.
  /think       — opposite: explicitly enable thinking, where the
                 default is off.

If you are migrating this prompt to a different model:
  - Strip every directive in this section.
  - Re-validate the prompt's behavior against the new model with
    /spp-loop, do not assume the directive's absence is harmless.
  - Document the strip in REPORT.md "Limitations" (DESIGN.md §2.2
    model-overfitting framing).

Leave this section empty if no model-specific directives apply.
-->

{{MODEL_DIRECTIVES}}
<!-- MODEL_DIRECTIVES: filled from loop_spec.md §5. May be empty.
     If non-empty, each directive should appear on its own line with
     a comment explaining its model-locked semantics. -->

<!--
============================================================
PROMPT BODY
============================================================
-->

<persona>
{{PERSONA_CONTENT}}
</persona>
<!-- PERSONA_CONTENT: who or what the model is being asked to
     embody for this task. One paragraph. Anchors the model in a
     consistent voice and judgment style. Filled during /spp-init.

     Example (generic): "You are a triage analyst evaluating
     incoming support tickets. You are thorough, calibrated, and
     willing to abstain when context is insufficient." -->

<task>
{{TASK_CONTENT}}
</task>
<!-- TASK_CONTENT: what the model should produce, in plain English.
     One short paragraph. Should not contain decision criteria —
     those live in <rules>. Filled during /spp-init.

     Example (generic): "Read the support ticket below and
     classify it as Relevant or Not Relevant to the billing team's
     queue. Return your classification in the format specified
     under <output_format>." -->

<rules>
{{RULES_CONTENT}}
</rules>
<!-- RULES_CONTENT: an enumerated list of decision criteria. This
     is the section that grows across iterations as the loop
     surfaces categorical edge cases. Each rule should be:
       - categorical (addresses a class of rows defined by an
         articulable property), not row-specific (DESIGN.md §10
         glossary).
       - testable (a labeler reading the rule and a row should be
         able to apply it without guessing intent).
       - non-redundant (rules should not partially overlap; if
         they do, merge them).

     Initial v01 rules can be sparse (3–5 entries). The loop will
     grow them. The auditor (DESIGN.md §4.2) reviews each new
     rule before iteration N+1 to keep this section's edits
     categorical, not row-specific.

     Example shape (generic, NOT real source-project content):
       1. Tickets that mention payment, invoice, charge, refund,
          or subscription billing are Relevant.
       2. Tickets that ask about feature availability without
          referencing a transaction are Not Relevant.
       3. Tickets that quote a third party's billing complaint
          without a first-person billing context are Not Relevant. -->

<output_format>
{{OUTPUT_FORMAT_CONTENT}}
</output_format>
<!-- OUTPUT_FORMAT_CONTENT: exactly what the model should emit.
     Short, structured, parseable. The eval pipeline parses this
     output to compute metrics, so format discipline is
     load-bearing.

     Example (generic):
       Return a JSON object with two fields:
         "label": one of {"Relevant", "Not Relevant"}
         "rationale": one short sentence (≤30 words)
       No surrounding prose. No markdown fences. -->

<example_input>
{{EXAMPLE_INPUT_CONTENT}}
</example_input>
<!-- EXAMPLE_INPUT_CONTENT: one representative input row, in the
     format the model will see at runtime. Generic data, not a
     real source-project row (DESIGN.md §7.2). -->

<example_output>
{{EXAMPLE_OUTPUT_CONTENT}}
</example_output>
<!-- EXAMPLE_OUTPUT_CONTENT: the correct output for the example
     input above, in the exact format from <output_format>. The
     example demonstrates compliance with the output contract,
     not edge-case reasoning. -->

<!--
============================================================
END OF PROMPT BODY
============================================================
-->

## Validation rules

A `prompt_v01.md` is **correctly filled** when all of the following
hold. The Phase 4 template linter verifies these mechanically.

1. All `{{...}}` placeholders are resolved.
2. The six XML sections appear exactly once each, in this order:
   `<persona>`, `<task>`, `<rules>`, `<output_format>`,
   `<example_input>`, `<example_output>`.
3. Each XML section has both an opening and a matching closing
   tag; no orphaned tags.
4. `<rules>` contains at least one enumerated rule (numbered list
   or bullet list).
5. `<example_input>` and `<example_output>` are non-empty and
   demonstrably correspond — applying the prompt's stated logic to
   the example input would produce the example output. This is a
   **manual review gate at PR time**, not a mechanical lint check;
   the linter cannot reason about whether a rule produces the
   illustrated output, only whether both sections are non-empty.
6. `<example_output>` complies with the format described in
   `<output_format>`.
7. Model-specific directives in the header (if any) are listed
   with comments explaining their model-locked semantics. The
   linter does not validate the directives themselves (model-
   specific) but does check that any directive line is followed
   by a comment.
8. No real data from a source project is reproduced in any
   placeholder content (DESIGN.md §7.2). This is a manual review
   gate at PR time, not a mechanical lint check.
